+## Lattice agreement between handoff divisions (measured)
+
+At 2,097,152 particles the lattice fixture's forces with 656 handoff blocks
+differ from those with 492 by 1.8e-5 in acceleration relative L2 (2.6e-3
+largest pointwise) and 4.4e-4 in potential relative L2, while the clustered
+and adaptive fixtures differ at about 6e-8 (Ewald-aware handoff, below). At
+492 the kernel evaluates the Ewald cell-cell interactions of the 512 box/8
+cells at the workitem roots; at 656 the CPU walk evaluates them. The
+difference is float rounding, not an error on either side: the lattice
+potential and forces are small residuals of sums of order N, and both
+evaluations carry the same rounding of those sums
+(`docs/cuda_kick_shared_memory_results/lattice_ewald_agreement/`).
+
+Reproduction. Pinning 512 or 1,024 handoff blocks moves the same work at
+32,768 particles (bucket 32) and 262,144 (bucket 128), where the box/8 cells
+hold 64 and 512 particles. An instrumented binary
+(`handoff_ewald_aware/counts/instrumentation.patch`) shows that 512 blocks
+hand the kernel the 512 box/8 cells carrying 10,752 and 27,212 Ewald checks.
+With 1,024 blocks no workitem carries Ewald checks, and the CPU walk
+evaluates 10,752 and 34,636 more Ewald cell-cell interactions
+(`counts_da3c1f4e/`). One process per case, lattice only, order 7, theta
+0.8, two threads, direct Ewald reference on 4,096 sinks (`run.sh`,
+`compare.py`). The 2,097,152-particle row is from the Ewald-aware handoff
+sweep:
+
+| particles | potential rel L2 | potential difference, mean / std | acceleration rel L2 (largest pointwise) |
+|---:|---:|---:|---:|
+| 32,768 | 2.4e-5 | 2.5e-4 / 2.1e-3 | 7.0e-6 (6.1e-4) |
+| 262,144 | 9.0e-5 | -1.5e-3 / 1.5e-2 | 1.5e-5 (1.2e-3) |
+| 2,097,152 | 4.4e-4 | | 1.8e-5 (2.6e-3) |
+
+The difference is position-dependent (its mean is a tenth of its spread),
+not a constant offset. It grows about fourfold per doubling of the side, as
+an absolute difference that is the same at every size would against the
+lattice's potential, which falls as the side grows. The export's units have
+particle mass 1, so the lattice potential is about 2.8 times the side (88.7
+at side 32).
+
+Cause. Reversing the order in which the CPU walk sums its Ewald cell-cell
+list (`diagnostics.patch`, `COSMICTIGER_DIAG_REVERSE_EWALD`, CPU-only runs)
+changes nothing but the rounding, and it moves the lattice by as much as the
+change of division does (`diagnostics.sh`):
+
+| fixture, particles | potential rel L2 | acceleration rel L2 | rms acceleration difference (rms acceleration) |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|
+| lattice, 32,768 | 4.7e-5 | 6.3e-6 | 4.0e-3 (632) |
+| lattice, 262,144 | 1.9e-4 | 1.2e-5 | 2.9e-2 (2,559) |
+| clustered, 32,768 | 2.8e-7 | 2.9e-8 | 3.5e-3 (120,500) |
+| adaptive, 32,768 | 4.4e-8 | 2.9e-9 | 3.6e-3 (1,257,000) |
+
+The absolute rounding is the same on every fixture. It looks large on the
+lattice only because the lattice's net field is small: its accelerations are
+200 to 2,000 times smaller than on the clustered and adaptive fixtures. The
+lattice potential is what remains of sums of order N, mostly the
+nearest-image sum of m/r, about 2.4 N m/L for a uniform box, less the Ewald
+corrections and the uniform background. One float rounding of 2.4 N against
+2.84 times the side (the Madelung constant of the simple cubic lattice) is
+5.1e-5, 2.1e-4 and 8.2e-4 at sides 32, 64 and 128. The
+measured differences between divisions are about half of that at every
+size. The clustered and adaptive fixtures differ at 6e-8 across the same
+handoffs because their potentials are close to their gross sums.
+
+Which side is right. Neither. Against the direct Ewald sum both divisions
+have the same errors to four digits: potential 3.769e-2 at 32,768 particles
+and 1.589e-1 at 262,144, acceleration 0.301 and 0.370. A CPU-only run has
+the same errors when it borrows the CUDA run's reference (the harness now
+saves it to `stf_walk_reference.csv`). The potential error is
+position-dependent too: mean -0.76 and spread 3.3 at side 32, -11.5 and 26 at
+side 64. That is about 4e-5 of the gross sum of m/r at both sizes, an
+ordinary truncation error that, like the rounding, looks large only against
+the lattice's small net potential. The spread of the difference between the
+divisions is under a thousandth of it.
+
+Source trace. The Ewald cell-cell interactions carry the quadratic of the
+uniform background, (2 pi / 3) m |x - y|^2, through traceless source
+moments, and the root expansion removes the background with the particles'
+own second moment. If each source's internal trace
+sum_i m_i |y_i - Y|^2 were lost, the potential would be off by
+(2 pi / 3) times a sum of traces that depends on how each sink's Ewald
+sources are cut, and not by a constant. Adding that trace back from the
+particles (`COSMICTIGER_DIAG_EWALD_TRACE`) moves the error mean from -0.76
+to +1,118 at side 32 and from -11.5 to +8,333 at side 64, so the expansion
+already accounts for it and nothing is lost.
+
+Ewald radial terms. Comparing the two operators found one defect, shared by
+both. `ewaldCorrectionDerivatives` evaluates the nearest image's
+erf(alpha r) / r radial terms d_l = r^l (d/(r dr))^l [erf(alpha r) / r]
+by an upward recurrence that cancels catastrophically for alpha r < 1. A
+float32 emulation of the generated code (`emulation/radial_terms.py`)
+puts d_4, d_5 and d_6 at the box/8 separation r = 1/8 wrong by 0.97, 85
+and 8,900 times their size. The generator now sums the power series of
+S_6(y) = int_0^1 t^12 exp(-y t^2) dt, y = alpha^2 r^2 <= 3, in twenty terms,
+recurs downward, S_(l-1) = (exp(-y) + 2 y S_l) / (2l - 1), which adds only
+positive terms, and forms d_l = (2 alpha / sqrt(pi)) (-2 alpha^2 r)^l S_l.
+The worst relative error of any d_l over 0.01 <= r <= 0.866 drops from
+6e28 to 1.4e-6 at order 7. `stf_ewald_cpu` now compares every rank of the
+float derivatives with the double ones at eight separations from 0.011 to
+0.71. The worst error against the rank's scale is 1.8e-6 at order 7 (1.2e-5
+at order 9) after the change, against 6.3e3 before (`unit_ratios/`). The
+change does not move the lattice difference between divisions: 2.37e-5 and
+8.81e-5 instead of 2.37e-5 and 8.99e-5. Nor does it move any fixture's
+error against the direct sum at orders 7 and 9 by more than 2e-4 of itself
+(`fixtures_reference.sh`, `fixtures/`), so on these fixtures the
+high-rank terms it corrects contributed below the rounding. The kick
+kernel keeps 255 registers, its 792-byte stack and its 202 local loads and
+731 local stores, and has 56 fewer instructions (37,760). TIMING_PLACEHOLDER
+
